mage":"","commonName":"mock orange 'Burfordensis'","nurseriesCount":3,"synonymParentPlantId":99581,"synonymParentPlantName":"<em>Philadelphus</em> 'Burfordensis'","isAgm":false,"isSynonym":true,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":44115,"botanicalName":"<em>Phillyrea</em> <em>angustifolia</em> f. <em>rosmarinifolia</em>","entityDescription":"A dense and compact evergreen shrub to 1.5m tall, with narrowly linear, dark green leaves. In early summer it produces small, creamy-white flowers, which although insignificant are pleasantly fragrant. A good plant for hedging or topiary","image":"rosem0221-125.jpg","commonName":"rosemary-leaved mock privet","nurseriesCount":3,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":315281,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'Emerald Green'","entityDescription":"A vigorous, tropical climber with glossy, heart-shaped, bright green leaves to 30cm long, held on long leaf stalks. Dark red, arum-type flowers may sometimes be produced on mature plants in summer","image":"","commonName":"ivy-leaved philodendron 'Green Emerald'","nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":521331,"synonymParentPlantName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>hederaceum</em> var. <em>hederaceum</em> 'Green Emerald'","isAgm":false,"isSynonym":true,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":388866,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'Florida Beauty'","entityDescription":"This is a hybrid of Philodendron pedatum and Philodendron squamiferum. These are both climbing species, and this trait is passed on to the hybrid. This cultivar bears heavily variegated foliage in yellow and green. The leaves on juvenile plants are spear-shaped, becoming deeply indented as the plant matures. It is still a rarity in cultivation and can be expensive to buy. The plant grows well as a houseplant and is undemanding in its care. Floral green spathes surrounding a white spadix or column, are rarely produced in cultivation and only on mature plants","image":"","commonName":"philodendron 'Florida Beauty'","nurseriesCount":2,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":526378,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'Florida Ghost' (v)","entityDescription":"This is a choice hybrid of Philodendron pedatum and Philodendron squamiferum. These are both climbing species, and this trait is passed on to the hybrid. This cultivar bears foliage which is pure white when young, becoming lime-green and then darker green as the leaves age. The leaves on juvenile plants are spear to arrow-shaped, becoming lobed and then deeply indented on either side of the central vein as the plant matures. It is still a rarity in cultivation and can be expensive to buy. The plant grows well as a houseplant and is undemanding in its care. It rarely flowers in cultivation but mature plants produce a reddish green spathe surrounding a light green spadix or column","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":2,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":510918,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'Mini Santiago'","entityDescription":"A miniature species of evergreen perennial vine found near to the town of Santiago, Ecuador, where it climbs on trees in tropical forests. The plant bears small, oval, mid-green, textured leaves, only 1-2cm across, with prominent veins. Its small size, rarely exceeding 60cm high and 45cm wide, slow growth and preference for high atmospheric humidity, make it an ideal species for growing in a terrarium. It has only recently been introduced and is still rare in cultivation but is becoming more available from specialist nurseries. Grown primarily for its foliage interest, flowers are unknown in cultivation","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":378520,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'Narrow Escape'","entityDescription":"A cultivar of Philodendron bipinnatifidum native to the tropical rainforests of northern South America where it climbs the trunks of forest trees using its aerial roots to cling to the bark. The elongated spear-shaped leaves are glossy green and have tooth-like indentations along the margins. The leaves can reach 90cm long on mature specimens. It is easy to grow in cultivation, adapting well to indoor conditions, making it useful as a houseplant, where it is valued for its ornamental foliage. Mature specimens can produce flowers consisting of a pale greenish-pink, sheath-like spathe, enclosing a white spadix or column","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":526132,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'Paraiso Verde' (v)","entityDescription":"A variegated, climbing cultivar of a currently unidentified species, that was collected from plants found growing on trees, in the rainforests of French Guyana. It has elongated, arrow-shaped leaves, with undulating margins. The rear lobes of the leaves tend to bend forwards. Their colour is a mottled, pale-yellow green, with randomly placed darker green markings. The leaves can be 60cm long. Mature plants can produce flowers consisting of a greenish-white, sheath-like spathe, surrounding a pale-pink spadix or column","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":272080,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'Pink Princess' (v)","entityDescription":"A tropical perennial with thick, clinging or trailing stems that are bright red when young. Leaves are oval-shaped with tapering tips, and glossy deep green splashed with cream and pink","image":"","commonName":"philodendron 'Pink Princess'","nurseriesCount":5,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":{"plantId":272080,"price":"£28.03","oldPrice":null,"url":"https://www.rhsplants.co.uk/plants/_/philodendron-pink-princess/classid.2000043218/sku.HP30001682/"}},{"id":526301,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> 'White Knight' (v)","entityDescription":"An evergreen perennial vine and variegated cultivar of Philodendron domesticum; a climbing species, native to Puerto Rico, where it grows against the trunks of trees in partially shaded, humid, tropical forests. This cultivar produces green and bright white, variegated, elongated, heart-shaped leaves, while the stems are deep red. Leaves on mature plants can be over 50cm long and 23cm wide. Plants can reach 1.5m high in cultivation. given the support of a moss pole on which to climb. Flowers rarely occur when grown indoors, but given ideal conditions, mature plants can produce a flower consisting of a sheath-like spathe, which is cream on the inside with a red base, and pale green on the outside. This surrounds a white spadix or column. Plants should be given partially shaded conditions, as hot, direct sun, can scorch the foliage","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":54678,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>angustisectum</em>","entityDescription":"An evergreen climber with aerial roots, to 5m in height, with glossy dark green leaves to 60cm in length, pinnately divided into narrow lobes. Pink-flushed green spathes 15cm in length produced occasionally","image":"WSY0037696_8418.jpg","commonName":"cut-leaved philodendron","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":522528,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>billietiae</em>","entityDescription":"A climbing, evergreen species native to Brazil, Guiana and French Guiana. Leathery, glossy, dark green, spear-shaped leaves with wavy, ridged leaf edges, up to 90cm long in mature specimens can be produced, each bearing distinctive orange-yellow leaf stalks. The leaf blades hang vertically. Plants can reach 1m high in cultivation, although much larger in habitat. It is relatively undemanding to grow as a house plant. Flowers may occasionally be produced on mature plants in cultivation in the warmer months. The spathe is orange-green and surrounds the spadix, or column, which is cream","image":"","commonName":"emerald vine philodendron","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":247855,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>burle-marxii</em>","entityDescription":"A climbing evergreen perennial vine growing up the trunks of trees in tropical forests of Colombia, Ecuador and Brazil. It produces glossy, elongated, heart-shaped, textured leaves to over 30cm long on mature plants. In cultivation it is a moderately fast grower, producing 30-60cm of new growth per year in ideal conditions. Plants can reach 1.2m high and across. It is reluctant to produce flowers in cultivation, but can produce flowers when mature and in ideal conditions, consisting of a sheath-like spathe, white on the inside, flushed red at the base, and green on the outside, which surrounds a white spadix or column. The foliage of the typical form is all green, while there is a variegated form in green and yellow","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":182569,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>cannifolium</em>","entityDescription":"A climbing species from the tropical rainforests of Brazil, where it grows against the trunks and branches in the understory of forest trees. The glossy green leaves are spear-shaped, with unusual swollen, spindle-shaped leaf stalks, hence its common name of fat boy. The plant can reach 90cm or so high indoors, and despite its provenance, adapts well to indoor conditions, although it is still rather rare in cultivation. On mature plants only, flowers can be produced consisting of a white, sheath-like spathe with darker burgundy colouration in the base, surrounding a white spadix or column","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":523124,"synonymParentPlantName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>martianum</em>","isAgm":false,"isSynonym":true,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":315280,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>corcovadense</em>","entityDescription":"A climbing species from the tropical rainforests of Brazil, where it grows against the trunks and branches in the understory of forest trees. The glossy green leaves are an elongated heart shape, and can reach 30cm long and 20cm across, with undulating edges. While easy to grow as a houseplant, despite its provenance, it is still rather rare in cultivation. Mature plants only, produce flowers consisting of a greenish-white, sheath-like spathe, surrounding a white spadix or column","image":"","commonName":null,"nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":47835,"botanicalName":"<em>Philodendron</em> <em>elegans</em>","entityDescription":"A vining species found growing in the rainforests of Colombia, using its aerial roots to cling to and climb the trees into their canopies. The glossy, green leaves are deeply indented resembling palm fronds on mature plants, but are more broadly lobe